!     detail
!         `Classfile(name)` reads the classpath resource at `name` — a JVM internal name with its
!         extension, `scala/Option.class` — through the `Classloader` given in scope, and
!         `Classfile[T]` finds the class of `T` by its `ClassTag` and reads the resource its
!         binary name denotes, so a nested class resolves to `Outer$Nested.class`. Both give
!         `Unset` when the classloader has no such resource.
-       `Classfile(data)` parses the bytes of a class file. `Classfile(name)` reads the classpath resource at `name` — a JVM internal name with its
-       extension, `scala/Option.class` — through the `Classloader` given in scope, and
-       `Classfile[T]` finds the class of `T` by its `ClassTag` and reads the resource its
-       binary name denotes, so a nested class resolves to `Outer$Nested.class`. Both give
-       `Unset` when the classloader has no such resource. All three raise `Classfile.Error`
+     detail
+         `Classfile(name)` lit la ressource du classpath située à `name` — un nom interne JVM avec
+         son extension, `scala/Option.class` — par le given `Classloader` dans la portée, et
+         `Classfile[T]` trouve la classe de `T` par son `ClassTag` et lit la ressource que désigne
+         son nom binaire ; une classe imbriquée se résout donc en `Outer$Nested.class`. Tous deux
+         donnent `Unset` lorsque le chargeur de classes n'a pas une telle ressource.

-       for bytes which do not parse.

-         Needs a `Tactic[Classfile.Error]`, raised if the code attribute is malformed.
